    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Flexion replaces the longer occipitofrontal diameter of the fetal\nskull with the shorter suboccipitobregmatic diameter, allowing\nthe smallest diameter of the head to pass through the maternal\npelvis. This process is related to flexion, not internal rotation.\nPositional changes of the presenting part during labor, known\nas cardinal movements, include:\n• Engagement\n• Descent\n• Flexion\n• Internal rotation\n• Extension\n• External rotation\n• Expulsion\n(Choice A) Engagement: Occurs when the fetal presenting\npart enters the pelvic inlet.\n(Choice B) Descent: The fetal head moves downward into the\npelvis, progressing toward delivery.\nDescent is the first requisite for vaginal birth. In nulliparas,\nengagement often occurs before labor onset, with further\ndescent typically happening during the second stage of labor.\n(Choice C) Flexion replaces the longer occipitofrontal\ndiameter of the fetal skull with the shorter\nsuboccipitobregmatic diameter. This allows the smallest\npresenting diameter of the head to present through the\nmaternal pelvis.\nFlexion occurs when the fetal chin tucks closer to the chest,\nresulting in flexion of the fetal head. This happens when the\ndescending fetal head meets resistance from the cervix, pelvic\nwalls, or pelvic floor.\n(Choice D) Internal Rotation: As the fetal vertex descends\ninto the midpelvis, it undergoes internal rotation from an OT\n(occiput transverse) position to align the sagittal suture with\nthe anteroposterior diameter of the pelvis, typically reaching\nthe occiput anterior (OA) position.",
