    "id": "medqbank-obstetrics-gynecology-0048",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 48,
    "stem": "All of the following are TRUE regarding\nsavage syndrome, EXCEPT:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "It causes primary amenorrhea"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "It is a hypothalamic disorder"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "It occurs due to ovarian\ninsufficiency"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "It results in hypergonadotropic\nhypogonadism"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "(Choice B) Savage syndrome is not a hypothalamic disorder,\nas the hypothalamus and pituitary function normally and are\ncapable of producing FSH and LH. The defect lies within\nthe ovaries themselves.\nSavage syndrome is characterized by a failure of the ovaries\nto respond to follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and\nluteinizing hormone (LH) secondary to a receptor defect.\n(Choice A) Savage syndrome leads to primary amenorrhea\nbecause it is a cause of primary ovarian\ninsufficiency (POI).\n(Choice D) Savage syndrome is a form of POI and is\nassociated with low estradiol levels but elevated FSH and LH\nlevels—a pattern known as hypergonadotropic\nhypogonadism.",
