    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0008",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 8,
    "stem": "A KOH smear of the vaginal swab can help in the diagnosis of",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Chlamydia trachomatis"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Bacterial vaginosis"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Trichomoniasis"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Vaginal candidiasis"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "A KOH smear is commonly used in the diagnosis of vaginal candidiasis (yeast infection). The test involves preparing a wet mount of vaginal discharge with potassium hydroxide (KOH), which helps in visualizing the characteristic pseudohyphae and budding yeast cells of Candida species.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "A KOH smear is not typically used for the diagnosis of Chlamydia trachomatis. Chlamydia is commonly diagnosed using nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs) or other specialized tests for detecting the DNA or RNA of the bacterium.",
      "B": "A KOH smear is not specific for bacterial vaginosis. Bacterial vaginosis is usually diagnosed based on clinical criteria, such as the Amsel criteria, or by microscopic examination of a Gram-stained vaginal smear, which reveals characteristic changes in the vaginal flora.\nA whiff test is performed by adding several drops of 10% potassium hydroxide to a sample of vaginal discharge. A strong fishy odor is indicative of a positive test result. Such a result may suggest either trichomoniasis or bacterial vaginosis.",
      "C": "A wet mount can be helpful in the diagnosis of trichomoniasis. Trichomonas vaginalis, the causative agent of trichomoniasis, is a flagellated protozoan.\nA whiff test is performed by adding several drops of 10% potassium hydroxide to a sample of vaginal discharge. A strong fishy odor is indicative of a positive test result. Such a result may suggest either trichomoniasis or bacterial vaginosis.",
      "D": "A KOH smear is commonly used in the diagnosis of vaginal candidiasis (yeast infection). The test involves preparing a wet mount of vaginal discharge with potassium hydroxide (KOH), which helps in visualizing the characteristic pseudohyphae and budding yeast cells of Candida species."
    },
