    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0009",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 9,
    "stem": "Colleen, 50 years old, G3P3 (2012) is diagnosed with cervical cancer Stage IIIB. the acceptable management is:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Radical hysterectomy"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Cervical conization"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Chemotherapy and radiotherapy"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Radical trachelectomy"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "This is the correct and often preferred management for Stage IIIB cervical cancer. Chemotherapy and radiotherapy are frequently used in combination for advanced stages of cervical cancer. Chemotherapy may be administered concurrently with radiotherapy to enhance treatment efficacy and address both local and systemic aspects of the disease.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the uterus, cervix, part of the vagina, and supporting tissues. However, at Stage IIIB, cervical cancer has typically spread beyond the cervix to nearby pelvic tissues and may involve the pelvic sidewall or lower third of the vagina. In such cases, surgery alone is often not the preferred treatment because the disease has advanced beyond what surgery can effectively address.",
      "B": "Cervical conization involves the removal of a cone-shaped piece of tissue from the cervix. While this procedure may be suitable for certain early-stage cervical cancers, it is not typically recommended for Stage IIIB cervical cancer, where the disease has spread extensively.",
      "C": "This is the correct and often preferred management for Stage IIIB cervical cancer. Chemotherapy and radiotherapy are frequently used in combination for advanced stages of cervical cancer. Chemotherapy may be administered concurrently with radiotherapy to enhance treatment efficacy and address both local and systemic aspects of the disease.",
      "D": "Radical trachelectomy is a surgical option that involves the removal of the cervix and surrounding tissues while preserving the uterus. This procedure is more commonly considered for early-stage cervical cancer and may not be the primary choice for Stage IIIB cases, where the disease has spread extensively."
    },
