    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0011",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 11,
    "stem": "Ultrasound scanning for congenital fetal anomaly is best requested on:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "12-16 weeks"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "30-32 weeks"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "16-20 weeks"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "20-24 weeks"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "This is correct.\nBetween 18 and 20 weeks gestation, most\npatients are offered a screening ultrasound. This provides the opportunity to screen for common fetal abnormalities. Also noted are the amniotic fluid volume, placental location, and GA.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "While an ultrasound during this time frame is often performed for various reasons, including confirming gestational age and assessing basic fetal anatomy, it is not typically the optimal time for detailed screening for congenital fetal anomalies. Detailed anatomy scans are usually performed later in the pregnancy.",
      "B": "At 30-32 weeks, the primary focus of ultrasound is often on assessing fetal growth, amniotic fluid levels, and placental function. It is not the preferred timing for the detailed screening of congenital fetal anomalies.",
      "C": "This is correct.\nBetween 18 and 20 weeks gestation, most\npatients are offered a screening ultrasound. This provides the opportunity to screen for common fetal abnormalities. Also noted are the amniotic fluid volume, placental location, and GA.",
      "D": "While still within the timeframe when an anatomy scan can be performed, the optimal window for this screening is typically between 18 and 20 weeks, as mentioned in the correct choice (c). This is the period when most anatomical structures are well visualized."
    },
