    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0035",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 35,
    "stem": "A maneuver done during delivery where the mother's thighs or legs are hyperflexed to her abdomen and abduction at the hips to facilitate delivery of the fetal shoulder:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Zavanelli Maneuver"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Piper's Maneuver"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Woodscrew Maneuver"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "McRoberts Maneuver"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "This is correct. McRoberts maneuver involves hyperflexing the mother's legs tightly to her abdomen. This widens the pelvis, and flattens the spine in the lower back (lumbar spine). If this maneuver does not succeed, an assistant applies pressure on the lower abdomen (suprapubic pressure), and the delivered head is also gently pulled. The technique is effective in about 42% of cases",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The Zavanelli maneuver is a rare and controversial obstetric maneuver performed during a shoulder dystocia situation. It involves the intentional replacement of the fetal head into the birth canal, followed by an emergency cesarean section. This maneuver is not specifically related to facilitating the delivery of the fetal shoulder.",
      "B": "Piper forceps are specialized forceps used only for the after-coming head of a breech presentation.",
      "C": "Wood’s Screw Maneuver leads to turning the anterior shoulder to the posterior and vice versa (somewhat the opposite of Rubin II maneuver)",
      "D": "This is correct. McRoberts maneuver involves hyperflexing the mother's legs tightly to her abdomen. This widens the pelvis, and flattens the spine in the lower back (lumbar spine). If this maneuver does not succeed, an assistant applies pressure on the lower abdomen (suprapubic pressure), and the delivered head is also gently pulled. The technique is effective in about 42% of cases"
    },
