    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0037",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 37,
    "stem": "Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism is treated with .",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Clomiphene to induce ovulation"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Hormonal replacement therapy"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Follicle stimulating hormone"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Gonadotropin releasing hormone agonist"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Hormonal replacement therapy (HRT) is a common and effective treatment for hypergonadotropic hypogonadism. In this condition, there is a deficiency of sex hormones (estrogen and progesterone in females, and testosterone in males). HRT aims to replace these hormones to alleviate symptoms and prevent long-term health complications.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Clomiphene is not typically used in the treatment of hypergonadotropic hypogonadism. Clomiphene is commonly used to induce ovulation in women with anovulatory conditions, particularly those with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS). It works by blocking estrogen receptors and promoting the release of follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH) from the pituitary gland.",
      "B": "Hormonal replacement therapy (HRT) is a common and effective treatment for hypergonadotropic hypogonadism. In this condition, there is a deficiency of sex hormones (estrogen and progesterone in females, and testosterone in males). HRT aims to replace these hormones to alleviate symptoms and prevent long-term health complications.",
      "C": "In some cases of hypergonadotropic hypogonadism, especially in women, supplementation with FSH may be considered. FSH is essential for the development and maturation of ovarian follicles in females.",
      "D": "Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonists are not typically used as a primary treatment for hypergonadotropic hypogonadism. GnRH agonists are more commonly employed in conditions such as precocious puberty or to suppress gonadal function in certain medical situations."
    },
