    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0051",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 51,
    "stem": "Which of the following will NOT support a diagnosis of ectopic pregnancy at the time of determination?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Presence of extrauterine gestational sac, hCG of 300 IU/ml"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Fluid in the cul-de-sac, hCG of 3,000 IU/ml"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "hCG of 300 IU/ml, no intrauterine gestational sac"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "No intrauterine pregnancy, hCG of 3,000 IU/ml"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "A low hCG level and the absence of an intrauterine gestational sac may suggest a possible ectopic pregnancy. However, the hCG level alone may not be conclusive, and additional findings would be considered.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "This scenario supports the possibility of an ectopic pregnancy. The presence of a gestational sac outside the uterus and a relatively low hCG level can be indicative of an ectopic pregnancy.",
      "B": "Fluid in the cul-de-sac, also known as free fluid or pelvic fluid, can be a sign of ectopic pregnancy. Additionally, the elevated hCG level further raises suspicion for an ectopic pregnancy.",
      "C": "A low hCG level and the absence of an intrauterine gestational sac may suggest a possible ectopic pregnancy. However, the hCG level alone may not be conclusive, and additional findings would be considered.",
      "D": "The absence of an intrauterine pregnancy combined with a higher hCG level raises concern for an ectopic pregnancy. The hCG level is relatively elevated, and the lack of an intrauterine gestational sac supports the suspicion."
    },
