    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0058",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 58,
    "stem": "In sperm analysis, the following are evaluated, EXCEPT:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Viscosity"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Sperm penetration assay"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Motility"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Sperm count"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "The sperm penetration assay is a test that evaluates the sperm's ability to penetrate a synthetic or hamster egg. It assesses the sperm's ability to fertilize an egg, providing information about sperm function. However, it is not routinely tested in semen analysis.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Viscosity refers to the thickness or stickiness of semen. It is typically assessed during semen analysis. Increased viscosity can affect sperm motility and the ability to swim effectively.",
      "B": "The sperm penetration assay is a test that evaluates the sperm's ability to penetrate a synthetic or hamster egg. It assesses the sperm's ability to fertilize an egg, providing information about sperm function. However, it is not routinely tested in semen analysis.",
      "C": "Sperm motility refers to the ability of sperm to move and swim. Motility is a crucial parameter in sperm analysis, as it reflects the sperm's ability to reach and fertilize an egg. Progressive motility (forward movement) is especially important for successful fertilization.",
      "D": "Sperm count, or sperm concentration, measures the number of sperm in a given volume of semen. It is a fundamental parameter in semen analysis and provides information about the quantity of sperm present."
    },
