    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0060",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 60,
    "stem": "Select the drug that inhibits the enzyme aromatase:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Clomiphene"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Danazol"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Quinestrol"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Letrozole"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Letrozole is an aromatase inhibitor. It works by inhibiting the activity of the aromatase enzyme, thus reducing the conversion of androgens to estrogens. Letrozole is commonly used in the treatment of hormone receptor-positive breast cancer in postmenopausal women.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Clomiphene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) that is used to induce ovulation in women with infertility. It does not inhibit aromatase; instead, it works by blocking estrogen receptors in the hypothalamus, leading to increased production of follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH).",
      "B": "Danazol is a synthetic androgen that has weak estrogenic and progestogenic activity. It is not an aromatase inhibitor. Danazol is sometimes used in the treatment of endometriosis and certain other conditions.",
      "C": "Quinestrol is an estrogen medication. It does not inhibit aromatase; instead, it provides exogenous estrogen to the body. It may be used in hormone replacement therapy (HRT) or other estrogen-related conditions.",
      "D": "Letrozole is an aromatase inhibitor. It works by inhibiting the activity of the aromatase enzyme, thus reducing the conversion of androgens to estrogens. Letrozole is commonly used in the treatment of hormone receptor-positive breast cancer in postmenopausal women."
    },
