    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0061",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 61,
    "stem": "Phocomelia is notable with which of the following teratogenic compounds?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Tetracycline"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Indomethacin"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Thalidomide"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Ethyl alcohol"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Thalidomide is notorious for causing phocomelia. It was initially prescribed as a sedative and antiemetic but led to severe limb abnormalities in babies born to mothers who took the drug during pregnancy. Thalidomide has since been restricted in its use and is only prescribed under strict conditions.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Tetracycline is an antibiotic and is not associated with phocomelia. However, it can cause tooth discoloration and affect bone growth when taken during pregnancy.",
      "B": "Indomethacin is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID). It is not known to cause phocomelia. However, NSAIDs, when used in the third trimester, may be associated with a condition called premature closure of the ductus arteriosus in the fetus.",
      "C": "Thalidomide is notorious for causing phocomelia. It was initially prescribed as a sedative and antiemetic but led to severe limb abnormalities in babies born to mothers who took the drug during pregnancy. Thalidomide has since been restricted in its use and is only prescribed under strict conditions.",
      "D": "Ethyl alcohol, or alcohol consumption during pregnancy, is associated with fetal alcohol syndrome (FAS), which can include a range of physical and developmental abnormalities. While limb abnormalities can occur in FAS, it is not the characteristic presentation of phocomelia."
    },
