    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0082",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 82,
    "stem": "Diana, 27 years old, G1P0 is 28 weeks with an abnormal OGTT but normal FBS, normal post prandial sugar levels. Her management should include the following .",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Insulin"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Pioglitazone"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Diet and exercise"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Glibenclamide"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Lifestyle modifications, including diet and exercise, are often the initial approach for managing gestational diabetes. For individuals with abnormal OGTT but normal FBS and postprandial levels, dietary adjustments and regular physical activity may be sufficient to control blood glucose levels.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Insulin therapy is generally reserved for cases of gestational diabetes when dietary and lifestyle modifications are insufficient to maintain blood glucose levels within the target range. However, in the absence of fasting hyperglycemia or elevated postprandial levels, initiating insulin may be overly aggressive and not the first-line choice.",
      "B": "Pioglitazone is not recommended for use in pregnancy due to potential adverse effects on fetal development.",
      "C": "Lifestyle modifications, including diet and exercise, are often the initial approach for managing gestational diabetes. For individuals with abnormal OGTT but normal FBS and postprandial levels, dietary adjustments and regular physical activity may be sufficient to control blood glucose levels.",
      "D": "Glibenclamide, an oral hypoglycemic agent, is not commonly used as a first-line treatment for gestational diabetes. It is generally reserved for cases where lifestyle modifications and insulin therapy are insufficient."
    },
