    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0083",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 83,
    "stem": "The primary hormone during the proliferative phase is .",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "hCG"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Estrogen"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "FSH"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Progesterone"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Estrogen is the dominant hormone during the proliferative phase. It is mainly produced by the developing ovarian follicles. Estrogen stimulates the thickening of the endometrial lining, increases vascularity, and promotes the growth of glandular tissue in the uterus.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Human Chorionic Gonadotropin (hCG) is primarily associated with pregnancy and the maintenance of the corpus luteum in early pregnancy. It is not the primary hormone during the proliferative phase of the menstrual cycle.",
      "B": "Estrogen is the dominant hormone during the proliferative phase. It is mainly produced by the developing ovarian follicles. Estrogen stimulates the thickening of the endometrial lining, increases vascularity, and promotes the growth of glandular tissue in the uterus.",
      "C": "Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH) plays a crucial role in the menstrual cycle by stimulating the development and maturation of ovarian follicles. However, during the proliferative phase, estrogen levels rise in response to FSH stimulation.",
      "D": "Progesterone becomes more prominent during the subsequent luteal phase, which follows the proliferative phase. It is produced by the corpus luteum after ovulation and is involved in preparing the endometrium for potential implantation."
    },
