    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0088",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 88,
    "stem": "On a cardiotocographic, there was note of abrupt decrease in fetal heart rate of greater than 15 beats below baseline lasting for at least 15 seconds but not more than 2 minutes in duration. The deceleration is not associated with uterine contractions. This is explained by:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Placental insufficiency"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Umbilical cord compression"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Compression of the fetal head"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Maternal hypertension"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "This is the correct answer.\nThe described pattern, characterized by an abrupt decrease in fetal heart rate (greater than 15 beats below baseline) lasting for at least 15 seconds but not more than 2 minutes, and not associated with uterine contractions, is indicative of variable decelerations.\nVariable decelerations are often caused by umbilical cord compression. Compression of the umbilical cord can transiently reduce blood flow to the fetus, leading to these decelerations.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Placental insufficiency typically manifests with late decelerations, which are gradual decreases in fetal heart rate associated with uterine contractions.",
      "B": "This is the correct answer.\nThe described pattern, characterized by an abrupt decrease in fetal heart rate (greater than 15 beats below baseline) lasting for at least 15 seconds but not more than 2 minutes, and not associated with uterine contractions, is indicative of variable decelerations.\nVariable decelerations are often caused by umbilical cord compression. Compression of the umbilical cord can transiently reduce blood flow to the fetus, leading to these decelerations.",
      "C": "This is not a common cause of variable decelerations. Variable decelerations are more often associated with cord compression.",
      "D": "Maternal hypertension can contribute to placental insufficiency, leading to late decelerations."
    },
