    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0093",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 93,
    "stem": "Mara, 30 year old G1P0, 8 weeks age of gestation, came in for vaginal spotting. There was no note of passage of meaty material per vagina. Quantitative hCG revealed 3,000 IU/ml. Transvaginal ultrasound shows the uterus measures 6x5x3.5cm, the endometrium is linear measuring 9mm. No adnexal mass seen. In addition to advising her, what will be your next step?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Do exploratory laparotomy"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Repeat hi-resolution ultrasound after 1 week"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Give methotrexate per orem"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Do dilatation and curettage"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "This option is a reasonable choice. In early pregnancy, especially when the gestational sac is small, it may not be possible to visualize the embryo or confirm viability. Repeating the ultrasound after a week can provide more information about the progression of the pregnancy.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Exploratory laparotomy is not indicated in this case. The patient's presentation is more suggestive of a possible early pregnancy complication, and surgical exploration is not the primary approach.",
      "B": "This option is a reasonable choice. In early pregnancy, especially when the gestational sac is small, it may not be possible to visualize the embryo or confirm viability. Repeating the ultrasound after a week can provide more information about the progression of the pregnancy.",
      "C": "Methotrexate is generally not used in cases of early pregnancy without clear evidence of ectopic pregnancy or specific indications for its use. It is not the appropriate initial management in this scenario.",
      "D": "Dilatation and curettage (D&C) is a procedure that involves dilating the cervix and scraping the uterine lining. In this case, with a small gestational sac and no evidence of an embryo, D&C is not the first-line management. It may be considered later if a nonviable pregnancy is confirmed."
    },
