    "id": "notion-obstetrics-gynecology-0094",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 94,
    "stem": "Which of the following is a risk factor for the development of macrosomia?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Multiple pregnancy"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Maternal obesity"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Preeclampsia"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Young primigravida"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Maternal obesity is a well-established risk factor for macrosomia. Women with a higher body mass index (BMI) are more likely to have larger babies. This association is partly due to factors such as increased maternal glucose levels and insulin resistance.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Multiple pregnancies (e.g., twins, triplets) is not a risk factor for macrosomia. The presence of more than one fetus can lead to decreased fetal growth.",
      "B": "Maternal obesity is a well-established risk factor for macrosomia. Women with a higher body mass index (BMI) are more likely to have larger babies. This association is partly due to factors such as increased maternal glucose levels and insulin resistance.",
      "C": "Preeclampsia is a hypertensive disorder that occurs during pregnancy and can lead to complications. While preeclampsia itself is not a direct cause of macrosomia, it can contribute to other factors that may affect fetal growth.",
      "D": "Young primigravida, referring to a woman who is pregnant for the first time at a young age, is not typically considered a direct risk factor for macrosomia."
    },
