    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "CIN 2 involves undifferentiated neoplastic cells that occupy more than one-third but less than two-thirds of the epithelium. It signifies a moderate degree of abnormality in the cervical cells.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "CIN (Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia) is a classification system used to describe abnormal changes in cervical cells. CIN 3 indicates moderate dysplasia. It involves the full thickness of the epithelium but does not breach the basement membrane.",
      "B": "CIN 3, not CIN 2, is typically associated with severe dysplasia and undifferentiated neoplastic cells. CIN 3 involves the full thickness of the epithelium and may extend beyond it, indicating a high-grade lesion.",
      "C": "HPV (Human Papillomavirus) infection is a major risk factor for the development of CIN. However, CIN itself is a histological classification that describes the degree of abnormality in cervical cells. CIN 2 indicates moderate dysplasia.",
      "D": "CIN 2 involves undifferentiated neoplastic cells that occupy more than one-third but less than two-thirds of the epithelium. It signifies a moderate degree of abnormality in the cervical cells."
    },
