    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 98,
    "stem": "18. A 52 year old female came in for complaints of\nhot flashes as it disrupts her sleep most days of the\nweek. She asks for your advise on management of\nher hot flashes. Which of the following is true?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Underweight women have more severe\nsymptoms"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "SSRI/SNRI are the mainstay nonhormonal\ntreatment of hot flashes"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "SSRI are FDA approved"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "lifestyle interventions do not decrease\nsymptoms"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "vaginal dryness is the primary reason women\nseek care during menopause"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "overweight women and those who smoke have\nmore severe symptoms. SSRI/SNRI are not FDA\napproved. Being in a cool environment is associated\nwith fewer subjective and objective hot flashes. Hot\nflashes is the primary reason women seek care\nduring menopause.\nBerek and Novak’s Gynecology 15th edition p.1235",
