    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 270,
    "stem": "A 38 weeks AOG G3P2 mother who has chronic\nhypertension presents to the ER due to vaginal\nbleeding associated abdominal pain and uterine\ncontractions. Physical examination reveals a firm,\ntender uterus with noted regular contractions\nevery 4-5 minutes and cervical dilatation of 4-5cm.\nBleeding has subsided and vital signs of the mother\nis stable. Fetal monitoring is reassuring.\nUltrasound reveals a high-lying placenta. How\nshould this patient be managed?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Stat cesarean delivery"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Give tocolytics"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Monitor progress of labor and do fetal status\nmonitoring"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Send the patient home"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "None of the above"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "A case of abruptio placenta. May be managed\nconservatively if vital signs are stable or fetal status\nis reassuring. If not, immediate delivery is\nnecessary",
