    "id": "superexam-obstetrics-gynecology-0314",
    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 314,
    "stem": "A 24 y/o sexually active nullipara went for consult\nsecondary to 4 days of fever and continuous lower\nabdominal pain. PE noted direct tenderness on (B)\nadnexal region without palpable mass. IE: (+)\ncervical motion tenderness. Speculum ex:\nhyperemic cervix with purulent discharge.\npregnancy test is positive, urinalysis shows are\nwithin normal limits, CBC: neutrophilic\nleukocytosis. pelvic ultrasound is unremarkable.\nwhat is the next best step in terms of management\n?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "IV clindamycin and gentamicin"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "IV ampicillin and gentamicin"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "IM spectinomycin and ceftriaxone"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "levofloxacin and metronidazole"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Laparoscopy"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "patient is suffering from severe PID and at the same\ntime, early pregnancy ( as noted by lack of UTZ\nfindings with (+) hcg). The most appropriate\ntreatment for this case is IV clindamycin and\ngentamicin. B, C- is ineffective since resistance is\ncommon in N gonnorrhea infection in the setting of\nPID. D- fluoroquinolone should never be given in\npregnant patient. furthermore, this combination\ntherapy is prescribed in milder cases of PID.",
