    "subject": "obstetrics-gynecology",
    "originalNumber": 367,
    "stem": "A 27 year old G3P2 on her 36th week of gestation,\nwith a history of chronic hypertension, had severe\nabdominal pains of several hours with\naccompanying vaginal bleeding. Vital signs are BP\n= 140/90, PR = 108bpm, RR = 22/min and T = 37.2",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Abdomen is tender and cervix is 3cm dilated,\nmembranes are bulging. CTG is non-reactive with\nhypertonic uterine contractions. The most likely\ndiagnosis is:"
      },
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Normal early labor"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Uterine rupture"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Placental abruption"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Placenta previa"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "None of the above"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Placental abruption presents with vaginal bleeding\nin 78 percent, uterine tenderness or back pain in 66\npercent, and fetal distress in 60 percent of patients.\nOther findings included frequent uterine\ncontractions and persistent uterine hypertonus.\nWilliams 23rd pg 765-766",
